INT. GENERATION SHIP - SEED VAULT - CYCLE 4,112

A circular chamber lined with frost-white drawers, each etched with a Latin name. Cold air hisses from vents. A single catwalk crosses the center, lit by thin blue strips.

ENGINEER NAKIA OKORO-VEGA, fifties, climbs the ladder to the catwalk, a tool belt clanking at her hip. Below, in a nest of cables, sits ARCHIVIST EZRA HADDAD, seventies, with a data slate propped on his knees.

She reaches the top and looks down at him. He doesn't look up.

NAKIA
Temperature's drifting.

EZRA
I know.

NAKIA
Drawer forty-one is already warm.

EZRA
I know.

She descends. He closes the slate. She kneels beside him and puts a hand on his shoulder, and he leans into it, barely.

NAKIA
How many are gone?

EZRA
Eleven thousand. Roughly.

NAKIA
Of how many?

EZRA
All of them, if she isn't fixed in a week.

She stands. She crosses to the nearest drawer, wipes frost from the label with her sleeve, and slides it open. Inside, tiny glass vials gleam in their rows, many clouded.

NAKIA
We can save the grains.

EZRA
The grains aren't the loss.

NAKIA
No?

EZRA
It's the orchids. The lilacs. The ones nobody needs.

She closes the drawer. She doesn't argue.

NAKIA
I'll start with the orchids.

EZRA
Thank you.

She climbs back to the catwalk, reaches into the ceiling panel, and begins to work. Below, he opens the slate and begins to read aloud the names of the dying, one by one.
